     "label": "authentication_model",
     "detail": "stdio transport: the client launches the server as a subprocess, so the OS process boundary is the auth boundary \u2014 `03` \u00a74 does not penalise absent auth here",
     "path": "",
     "line": 0,
     "excerpt": "",
     "url": "",
     "finding": "",
     "severity": "",
     "first_seen": null,
     "deducts": true
    },
    {
     "label": "transport_security",
     "detail": "declared transport: stdio \u2014 launched as a subprocess by the client, so the security boundary is the client process, not a network port (`03` \u00a74)",
     "path": "",
     "line": 0,
     "excerpt": "",
     "url": "",
     "finding": "",
     "severity": "",
     "first_seen": null,
     "deducts": true
    },
    {
     "label": "secret_handling",
     "detail": "detect-secrets matched 3 credential-shaped string(s), every one from a keyword or entropy heuristic (Base64 High Entropy String, Secret Keyword). Those flag placeholders, variable names and content hashes as readily as credentials, so they do not establish the committed credential `03` \u00a74's 0 asserts, and this sub-check is not scored; Secret Keyword at docs/Design/SRD0075.md:39; Base64 High Entropy String at sqlprojects/AW/Tables/BillOfMaterials.sql:24; Secret Keyword at sqlprojects/TSQLSmellsTest/HardCodedCredentialsTest.sql:7",
     "path": "",
     "line": 0,
     "excerpt": "",
     "url": "",
     "finding": "",
     "severity": "",
     "first_seen": null,
     "deducts": true
    },
    {
     "label": "authorization_granularity",
     "detail": "no tool registration was recognised anywhere in the source. An MCP server registers tools by definition, so this is a gap in our detection rather than a server with none, and `03` \u00a74's threshold cannot be applied to it.",
     "path": "",
     "line": 0,
     "excerpt": "",
     "url": "",
     "finding": "",
     "severity": "",
     "first_seen": null,
     "deducts": true
    }
